                <pb facs="0814" n="803" sameAs="frus:frus1937v02/pg803"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.822/15: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Walton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-16T17:00:00-00:44">December 16, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 16—2:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>75. Department’s telegram No. 37, December 11, 3 p.m.<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d607-f1"/> In connection with the Department’s instruction No. 61,
                    November 15, Liberian Government accepts counterproposals but desires to know
                    whether or no article 14 implies to waive port and harbor dues on American
                    vessels calling at Liberian ports and vice versa. If it does not, Liberian
                    Government is ready to conclude and sign commercial treaty.</p>
                <p>In connection with Department’s telegram No. 29, Nov. 18,<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d607-f2"/> Liberian Government has accepted counterproposals.<anchor
                        n="18" xml:id="d607-f3"/> I am awaiting instructions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Walton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
